Article 62

1. In order to ensure the effectiveness of implementing CFC policy in the Region, a CFC Task Force is established.
2. The establishment and membership composition of the CFC Task Force shall be stipulated by Regent's Decree.
3. The CFC Task Force is domiciled at the Office of Women's Empowerment and Family Planning.
4. The CFC Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) has the main duties of: a. coordinating the implementation of CFC policy and development;
b. conducting socialization, advocacy and communication, information and education of CFC policy;
c. collecting basic data for CFC development;
d. conducting needs analysis sourced from basic data;
e. disseminating basic data;
f. determining the focus and priority of programs in realizing CFC, adjusted to regional potential;
g. preparing a 5 (five) year Child-Friendly City regional action plan and work mechanism; and h. conducting monitoring, evaluation and reporting at least once (1) a year.

Article 11

1. The Gender Mainstreaming Focal Point in each SKPD shall consist of officials and/or staff responsible for planning and/or programs.  (2) The Gender Mainstreaming Focal Point as referred to in paragraph (1) has the duties of:a. promoting Gender Mainstreaming in the work unit;
b. facilitating the preparation of gender responsive SKPD work plans and budgeting;
c. conducting training, socialization, advocacy of Gender Mainstreaming to all officials and staff within the SKPD;
d. reporting the implementation of Gender Mainstreaming to the SKPD leadership;
e. encouraging the implementation of gender analysis of policies, programs, and activities in the work unit; andf. facilitating the preparation of gender data in each SKPD;  (3) The Gender Mainstreaming Focal Point as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be selected and appointed by the SKPD leadership.

Article 13

Efforts to enhance the role, quality and capacity of women in the legal field as referred to in Article 5 paragraph (1) letter d number 2 include:  a. conducting socialization to increase women's legal knowledge and awareness;
b. mapping and reviewing gender-biased policies;
c. involving women's organizations in the process of drafting and discussing Regional legal products related to women's participation;

Article 17

In order to prevent acts of Violence against women, the Regional Government:
a. conducts socialization regarding the prevention of Violence against women by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. builds networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children victims of Violence;
c. provides public facilities that are comfortable and safe for women;

Article 67

1. In order to prevent violations of women's rights, the community: a. monitors various conditions occurring in their environment that may cause violations of women's rights;

b. increases awareness of violations of women's rights;
c. strengthens the role of community, religious and family environment organizations;

d. supervises the implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection organized by the Regional Government through a mechanism for conveying aspirations to the Regent or regional people's representative council;

2. In order to prevent violations of Children's rights, the community: a. monitors various conditions occurring in their environment that cause violations of Children's rights;

b. increases awareness of violations of Children's rights;
c. strengthens the role of community, religious and family environment organizations; and prevents early marriage.

Article 68

The family in efforts to Protect women's rights plays a role in: a. protecting women from conflict and Violence in the household that degrades the dignity of women;
b. providing equal opportunities for women to develop themselves in the fields of education, social, economic, political and cultural matters; and c. preventing violations of women's rights in the household.

Article 14

Efforts to enhance the role, quality and capacity of women in the social field as referred to in Article 5 paragraph (1) letter d number 3 include:  a. enhancement of knowledge and skills to encourage the fulfillment of education in accordance with existing potential;
b. enhancement and fulfillment of health quality for women;
c. enhancement of awareness and knowledge about independent, healthy and prosperous family planning including access to consultation services and marriage registration;
d. enhancement of women's awareness of the impact of early marriage;
e. facilitating and promoting the preservation of customs and the development of science, technology, arts and culture for the advancement of women;
f. enhancing women's awareness and knowledge of independent, healthy and prosperous family planning;
g. implementing programs to reduce maternal mortality rates;
h. encouraging the establishment of women's organizations in the Region;

Article 9

The Regional Gender Mainstreaming working group as referred to in Article 14 has the duties of:
a. promoting and facilitating Gender Mainstreaming to each SKPD;
b. conducting socialization and advocacy of Gender Mainstreaming to sub-district heads and wali nagari;
c. preparing work programs every year;
d. encouraging the realization of gender Responsive planning and budgeting;
e. preparing the work plan of the Gender Mainstreaming working group every year;
f. being accountable to the Regent through the Vice Regent;
g. formulating policy recommendations to the Regent;
h. preparing the Regional Gender profile;
i. monitoring the implementation of Gender Mainstreaming in each agency;
j. establishing a technical team to conduct analysis of the Regional budget;
k. preparing a regional action plan for Regional Gender Mainstreaming; and  l. encouraging the implementation of selection and appointment of focal points in each SKPD.

Article 37

Guidance as referred to in Article 35 letter b includes:
a. conducting counseling on improving women's quality of life;
b. conducting premarital counseling;

Article 12

Efforts to enhance the role, quality and capacity of women in the political field as referred to in Article 5 paragraph (1) letter d number 1 include:  a. conducting socialization to increase women's political participation in general elections;
b. implementing programs that encourage the level of women's involvement in political processes and public office;
c. facilitating women's self-development in organizing, associating and assembling;
d. involving women in decision-making concerning women's interests;

Article 16

1. The Regional Government in its efforts to Protect women is responsible for:
a. establishing and implementing policies, programs and activities for the Protection of women;
b. making efforts to prevent acts of Violence against women including criminal acts of trafficking in persons;
c. increasing the effectiveness of services for women victims of acts of Violence;
d. increasing efforts for the Protection of female workers;
e. strengthening and developing institutions providing Protection services for women;
f. conducting cooperation with the Government, other Regional Governments, the private sector, business world and/or NGOs in efforts to prevent acts of Violence against women;
g. involving the active role of families, ninik mamak, bundo kanduang, customary leaders, religious leaders, the community, and institutions in the field of Protection of women;
2. The responsibility of the Regional Government in efforts to Protect women as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented by SKPD in accordance with their duties and functions and may coordinate with related agencies.

Article 2

The implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection is based on the principles of:
a. best interests of women and Children;
b. gender justice and gender equality;
c. local wisdom;
d. law enforcement and legal certainty.
e. non-discrimination;
f. right to life, survival, and development;
g. respect for the opinion of Children; and
h. law enforcement and legal certainty.

Article 5

1. In order to improve the quality of life of women, the regional government is responsible for:a. respecting and guaranteeing the rights of women without discrimination in accordance with human dignity;
b. implementing Gender Mainstreaming in the Region;
c. establishing a Gender Mainstreaming working group in the Region;
d. enhancing Women's Empowerment in roles, quality and capacity in the fields of;
1. politics;
2. law;
3. social; and
4. economy.  e. strengthening and developing service provider institutions for Women's Empowerment in the Region;
2. The responsibility of the Regional Government in efforts to improve the quality of life of women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by SKPD in accordance with their duties and functions.

Article 3

This Regional Regulation aims to:
a. guarantee the fulfillment of the rights of women and Children in the Region  b. serve as a guideline for the Region in planning, policies and strategies for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;
c. protect and prevent women and Children from actions, decisions and policies that violate the rights of women and Children;
d. enhance the role and value of local wisdom as well as the role of customs in Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;

Article 18

1. Protection of women victims of acts of Violence is implemented through integrated services.
2. The provision of integrated services for women victims of acts of Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) uses the principles of:
a. gender responsiveness;
b. non-discrimination;
c. equal and respectful relationships;
d. maintaining confidentiality;
e. providing a sense of security and comfort;
f. respecting individual differences;
g. non-judgmental;

h. respecting the choices and decisions of victims;
i. sensitivity to the background and condition of victims and the use of language that is appropriate and understood by victims;
j. fast and simple;
k. empathy; and
l. fulfillment of women's rights.

Article 4

The scope of regulation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection includes:
a. improvement of women's quality of life;
b. Protection of women;
c. gender and Child data system;
d. fulfillment of Children's rights;
e. Child Protection;
f. community participation; andg. guidance and supervision.

Article 49

1. Promotive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 46 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at improving health status more optimally.
2. Promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by providing socialization or counseling to the community regarding healthy lifestyles which include:a. the importance of exclusive breastfeeding for infants;
b. the importance of immunization for toddlers;
c. clean living patterns;
d. the importance of nutritious food;
e. the dangers of drugs and smoking; and (3) In addition to promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (2), Regional Government may carry out other activities in the context of health implementation.

