h. preparing the Regional Gender profile;
i. monitoring the implementation of Gender Mainstreaming in each agency;
j. establishing a technical team to conduct analysis of the Regional budget;
k. preparing a regional action plan for Regional Gender Mainstreaming; and  l. encouraging the implementation of selection and appointment of focal points in each SKPD.

Article 10

1. The Technical Team as referred to in Article 9 letter j shall consist of officials who understand gender responsive budget analysis.
2. The regional action plan for Regional Gender Mainstreaming as referred to in Article 9 letter k shall contain:a. Gender Mainstreaming in legislation in the Region;
b. Gender Mainstreaming in the development cycle in the Region;
c. institutional strengthening of Gender Mainstreaming in the Region; and  d. strengthening community participation in the Region.

Article 49

1. Promotive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 46 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at improving health status more optimally.
2. Promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by providing socialization or counseling to the community regarding healthy lifestyles which include:a. the importance of exclusive breastfeeding for infants;
b. the importance of immunization for toddlers;
c. clean living patterns;
d. the importance of nutritious food;
e. the dangers of drugs and smoking; and (3) In addition to promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (2), Regional Government may carry out other activities in the context of health implementation.

Article 5

1. In order to improve the quality of life of women, the regional government is responsible for:a. respecting and guaranteeing the rights of women without discrimination in accordance with human dignity;
b. implementing Gender Mainstreaming in the Region;
c. establishing a Gender Mainstreaming working group in the Region;
d. enhancing Women's Empowerment in roles, quality and capacity in the fields of;
1. politics;
2. law;
3. social; and
4. economy.  e. strengthening and developing service provider institutions for Women's Empowerment in the Region;
2. The responsibility of the Regional Government in efforts to improve the quality of life of women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by SKPD in accordance with their duties and functions.

Article 42

1. Regional Government in efforts to fulfill Children's rights is responsible for:
a. establishing and implementing policies and programs/activities for the fulfillment of Children's rights;
b. respecting and guaranteeing the human rights of every Child without distinguishing ethnicity, religion, race, class, gender, ethnic group, culture and language, legal status of the Child, birth order of the Child, and physical and/or mental condition;
c. strengthening and developing service provider institutions for improving family quality in realizing Children's rights;
d. guaranteeing the Protection, care, and welfare of Children with due regard to the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, or other persons legally responsible for the Child;
e. providing facilities and infrastructure in the implementation of the fulfillment of Children's rights;
f. guaranteeing the right of Children to express opinions in accordance with the age and level of intelligence of the Child;
g. conducting supervision and guidance in the context of implementing the fulfillment of Children's rights;

h. cooperating with private parties and/or NGOs in the implementation of Child Protection;

2. The responsibility of the Regional Government in Child Protection efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by SKPD in accordance with their duties and functions.

Article 16

1. The Regional Government in its efforts to Protect women is responsible for:
a. establishing and implementing policies, programs and activities for the Protection of women;
b. making efforts to prevent acts of Violence against women including criminal acts of trafficking in persons;
c. increasing the effectiveness of services for women victims of acts of Violence;
d. increasing efforts for the Protection of female workers;
e. strengthening and developing institutions providing Protection services for women;
f. conducting cooperation with the Government, other Regional Governments, the private sector, business world and/or NGOs in efforts to prevent acts of Violence against women;
g. involving the active role of families, ninik mamak, bundo kanduang, customary leaders, religious leaders, the community, and institutions in the field of Protection of women;
2. The responsibility of the Regional Government in efforts to Protect women as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented by SKPD in accordance with their duties and functions and may coordinate with related agencies.

CHAPTER VII

CHILD PROTECTION

Article 64

The Regional Government, in order to provide protection to Children: a. implements and supports national policy in organizing Child Protection in the Region;
b. guarantees Children to exercise their rights in expressing opinions according to the age and intelligence level of the Child;
c. conducts prevention of Violence against Children by optimizing the role of customary institutions;
d. conducts socialization, seminars and counseling regarding Protection and Children's rights;
e. develops child-friendly school models;
f. provides public facilities that are friendly and safe for Children g. encourages public awareness of the importance of Protection for Children;

Article 58

1. Facilitation of the Child justice system includes criminal and civil justice.
(2) Facilitation of the criminal justice system as referred to in paragraph (1) includes: a. provision of companions or Child volunteers during examination by investigators;
b. provision of legal and psychological assistance services;
c. involving members/investigators of the local police in efforts to prevent criminal acts committed by Children as well as Children as victims;
d. realizing restorative justice processes for Children in conflict with the law; and e. rehabilitation and social reintegration services;
3. Fulfillment of Children's rights in the civil justice system as referred to in paragraph (1) includes the provision and delivery of consultation and legal assistance services during the judicial process.
4. In facilitating the Child justice system as referred to in paragraphs (1) and (2), the authorized SKPD is the SKPD responsible for the Protection of Women and Children

Article 62

1. In order to ensure the effectiveness of implementing CFC policy in the Region, a CFC Task Force is established.
2. The establishment and membership composition of the CFC Task Force shall be stipulated by Regent's Decree.
3. The CFC Task Force is domiciled at the Office of Women's Empowerment and Family Planning.
4. The CFC Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) has the main duties of: a. coordinating the implementation of CFC policy and development;
b. conducting socialization, advocacy and communication, information and education of CFC policy;
c. collecting basic data for CFC development;
d. conducting needs analysis sourced from basic data;
e. disseminating basic data;
f. determining the focus and priority of programs in realizing CFC, adjusted to regional potential;
g. preparing a 5 (five) year Child-Friendly City regional action plan and work mechanism; and h. conducting monitoring, evaluation and reporting at least once (1) a year.

Article 14

Efforts to enhance the role, quality and capacity of women in the social field as referred to in Article 5 paragraph (1) letter d number 3 include:  a. enhancement of knowledge and skills to encourage the fulfillment of education in accordance with existing potential;
b. enhancement and fulfillment of health quality for women;
c. enhancement of awareness and knowledge about independent, healthy and prosperous family planning including access to consultation services and marriage registration;
d. enhancement of women's awareness of the impact of early marriage;
e. facilitating and promoting the preservation of customs and the development of science, technology, arts and culture for the advancement of women;
f. enhancing women's awareness and knowledge of independent, healthy and prosperous family planning;
g. implementing programs to reduce maternal mortality rates;
h. encouraging the establishment of women's organizations in the Region;
